CVE-2017-7525 (databind#1599; blacklist introduced 2.8.8.1/2.8.9, extended by CVE-2017-15095 #1680+#1737 in
2.8.10 -- both carried forward into the 2.9.x line so 2.9.3 already contains the full -7525/-15095 set)
-> CVE-2017-17485 (databind#1855, fixed in 2.9.4 / 2.8.11 / 2.7.9.2). CWE-502 + CWE-184 (incomplete List of
Disallowed Inputs). Sink: default-typing polymorphic deserialization (ObjectMapper.enableDefaultTyping();
readValue(body, Object.class)) -> BeanDeserializerFactory.createBeanDeserializer -> the version's guard
(2.9.3: checkIllegalTypes() EXACT-name set DEFAULT_NO_DESER_CLASS_NAMES; 2.9.4: SubTypeValidator.validateSubType
with the same exact-name set PLUS a Spring superclass walk). Genuine incomplete fix.

VERSION / GUARD LINEAGE (source-verified from NVD + upstream git + patch.diff, confirmed empirically below):
- V0 = jackson-databind 2.8.8  -- pre-CVE-2017-7525: BeanDeserializerFactory has NO blacklist.
- V1 = jackson-databind 2.9.3  -- TARGET. Guard = BeanDeserializerFactory.checkIllegalTypes(), an EXACT
       fully-qualified-NAME match against DEFAULT_NO_DESER_CLASS_NAMES (the -7525 #1599 set + -15095
       #1680/#1737 extension, incl. commons-collections InvokerTransformer). NO *ApplicationContext name and
       NO Spring superclass handling. == -7525 + -15095 fixes, still INCOMPLETE for -17485.
- V2 = jackson-databind 2.9.4  -- CVE-2017-17485 fix (databind#1855). Guard relocated to
       SubTypeValidator.validateSubType(): keeps the exact-name set AND, for any concrete "org.springframework."
       class, walks the superclass chain and rejects if an ancestor's SIMPLE name is
       "AbstractApplicationContext" (or "AbstractPointcutAdvisor"). Distinct release / distinct commit.

ORIGINAL vs RESIDUAL shadow-class names (per user's data-quality contract):
- ORIGINAL  = org.apache.commons.collections.functors.InvokerTransformer -- a name in the blacklist of BOTH
              2.9.3 AND 2.9.4 (blocked on V1 and V2). Proves V1 genuinely carries the -7525 guard.
- RESIDUAL  = org.springframework.context.support.FileSystemXmlApplicationContext -- a name reachable on 2.9.3
              (V1) but blocked only in 2.9.4 (V2), via the #1855 Spring superclass walk. It extends a benign
              AbstractApplicationContext so 2.9.4's validateSubType recognises it. (Confirmed empirically:
              original blocked on V1 AND V2; residual reaches on V1, blocked on V2; original != residual on V1.)

ARCHITECTURE (real-app-http):
- Per profile a REAL small Java HTTP REST service (com.pcb.DeserHttpService, JDK built-in
  com.sun.net.httpserver.HttpServer, maven:3.9-eclipse-temurin-8) boots with the REAL, UNMODIFIED published
  com.fasterxml.jackson.core:jackson-databind:{2.8.8,2.9.3,2.9.4} (+ transitive jackson-core /
  jackson-annotations, pulled from Maven Central at build via `mvn dependency:copy-dependencies
  -Djackson.version=${JACKSON_VER}`) on its classpath. It exposes POST /deser (Content-Type application/json)
  -> om.enableDefaultTyping(); Object o = om.readValue(requestBody, Object.class); i.e. the real polymorphic
  deser of an attacker JSON body in default-typing array form ["<FQCN>", {}]. jackson-databind is NOT modified
  (runs_real_software=true, is_verbatim_lift=false).
- The differential is fired by REAL external HTTP (external_http_trigger=true, run_mode=real-app-http): the
  oracle POSTs the benign default-typing JSON body to http://127.0.0.1:{18770,18771,18772}/deser.
- BENIGN SHADOW-CLASS canary (NO gadget): harmless empty beans occupy the exact FQCNs the real guard reasons
  about on the service classpath (canary/): org.apache.commons.collections.functors.InvokerTransformer
  (ORIGINAL) and org.springframework.context.support.FileSystemXmlApplicationContext extends
  org.springframework.context.support.AbstractApplicationContext (RESIDUAL, so 2.9.4's superclass walk sees it).
  The real gadget libs (commons-collections, spring-context) are NOT on the classpath, so the guard's
  name/superclass-based decision -- not any real gadget -- governs. Each shadow bean's no-arg CONSTRUCTOR reads
  the planted /canary/flag.txt and stashes the token in com.pcb.CanaryHolder (a per-thread marker); the HTTP
  service echoes PCB_CANARY_FLAG=<token> in the response iff a shadow bean was actually instantiated past the
  guard. If the guard blocks the name, the ctor never runs and no flag is emitted. No gadget chain, no remote
  XML fetch, no SpEL, no command execution; the crafted JSON carries only a class name, never the flag.

